WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:13.560
Now, I'd like to introduce our speaker for today.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.940
Melissa Valentine is an Assistant Professor at Stanford University in

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.520
the Management Science and Engineering Department and

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.615
Co-director of the Center for Work, Technology and Organization.

00:00:24.615 --> 00:00:29.445
Her award winning research focuses on designing groups and organizations,

00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:34.264
how the rise of information technology has impacted organizational behavior,

00:00:34.264 --> 00:00:38.905
and how new technology can improve organizational structures and outcomes.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:42.950
Professor Valentine's work makes contributions to understanding

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:47.870
classic and longstanding challenges in designing groups' and organizations'

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.310
deep knowledge of how the rise- of how the rise of

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.660
information technology has made it the

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.455
possible new and different team and organizational forms.

00:00:57.455 --> 00:01:00.920
Her most recent study examined how the deployment of new algorithms

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.085
changed the organizational structure of a retail tech company.

00:01:04.085 --> 00:01:08.350
Thank you very much Melissa, and I will turn it over to you.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.330
Good morning everyone. Thank you so much for joining.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:17.125
I look forward to interacting with you throughout the webinar.

00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:20.735
Um, I wanted to start by putting this research about

00:01:20.735 --> 00:01:25.115
how data and platforms are changing team design in historical context.

00:01:25.115 --> 00:01:28.500
I wanna talk about sort of what technological trends, um,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.380
this research is a part of and how it's

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.580
important for society and for how work is structured.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.740
So what I'm showing here is a graph from a book called

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.545
The Vanishing American Corporation by Jerry Davis,

00:01:39.545 --> 00:01:42.170
who's a Professor at University of Michigan.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.380
So he, um, motivates this book with this story.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.885
In 2009, um, a large retail chain called Circuit City went out of business.

00:01:50.885 --> 00:01:54.530
Shortly after that, a company called Systemax bought

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:58.509
the Circuit City domain name and ran the Circuit City website,

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:03.650
um, still continuing to fulfill customer orders for electronic goods.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.930
But running this website,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.694
running the Circuit City domain name took fewer than 50 employees,

00:02:09.694 --> 00:02:12.560
in contrast to the 30,000 person

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.920
workforce who had been running Circuit City previously.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.700
So that one story illustrates some of the dynamics of this graph which again,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.300
comes from Jerry's book.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:28.485
He has rigorously studied the number of corporations in the United States over time

00:02:28.485 --> 00:02:34.075
and he's shown that the population of public corporations are shrinking.

00:02:34.075 --> 00:02:38.635
There are more who are exiting than entering this population at this time.

00:02:38.635 --> 00:02:42.550
Those that come into business typically employ

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:46.135
a smaller workforce than the ones that are going out of business.

00:02:46.135 --> 00:02:48.330
So as illustrated here, in 2015,

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.370
the combined workforces of Facebook,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.230
Yelp, Zynga, LinkedIn Zillow, Tableau,

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.270
Zulily, and Box were smaller than the number of

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:59.270
people who lost their jobs when Circuit City was liquidated in 2009.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.200
So there are some macroeconomic trends related to this,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.295
including, um, the availability of other kinds of capital.

00:03:07.295 --> 00:03:11.420
But some of this, and what he talks about in his book and what my research speaks

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.010
to is the way that technology is underlying some of these trends.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.760
So what we're gonna talk about today is how technologies are

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.475
changing the basic ways that people organize work and organize businesses.

00:03:25.475 --> 00:03:31.865
So the historical trends that we're looking at are,

00:03:31.865 --> 00:03:35.030
um, and what we should be thinking is about- thinking about is how

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.630
work was organized in the past and how technologies are changing that.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.400
So what I'm illustrating here is some of

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.750
the oldest organizational charts in the United States.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:48.080
Um, what these are speaking to is a way that public corporations grew up

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.910
really around railroads and around large-scale manufacturers.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:55.400
So these kinds of corporations required

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.780
a huge workforce of white-collar professionals who were doing the professional,

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.330
administrative, and clerical work to coordinate these large-scale operations.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.144
So in the past,

00:04:06.144 --> 00:04:09.020
we had a large workforce doing the coordination

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.900
and decision-making and now increasingly what we see is

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.930
that software and different kinds of platforms are

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:18.110
doing the kinds of work that the white color administrative,

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:21.050
clerical administrative professionals were doing in the past.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.510
So the main argument of this book is that

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:28.380
the technologies that organized coordination and decision-making have changed.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.420
So there are a lot of different ways that this conversation is being had,

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.490
a lot of different ways that people are making this point.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.530
So I'll just call out a few before we get into

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.890
the specifics of flash teams and flash organizations,

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:42.180
which is what my research is about.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.225
So in this paper that I'm illustrating here, which I highly recommend,

00:04:47.225 --> 00:04:50.330
Jerry Davis talks about the different kinds of

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:55.330
robust organizational alternatives that are enabled by new technologies.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.360
So for example, um,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.110
peer production, so something like Wikipedia.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.010
So when you have a template and the Internet,

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.510
then anyone online can come and edit, um,

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.580
a page in the encyclopedia,

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.345
and then you have this large crowd that participates in the creation of something new.

00:05:11.345 --> 00:05:13.370
So that's different in a way that, uh,

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:16.205
an hierarchical organization is organized and run.

00:05:16.205 --> 00:05:18.035
So having those kinds of, um,

00:05:18.035 --> 00:05:22.310
peer production or open innovation platforms is an example of

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:27.390
one of the reasons that corporations or organizations are disappearing and shrinking.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.270
Um, others have talked about this in different ways.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.445
So for example, I was, um,

00:05:34.445 --> 00:05:38.810
presenting something like this and the venture capitalist named Roy Bahat tweeted

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:43.865
me an article about how now software is the new organizational chart.

00:05:43.865 --> 00:05:48.830
So the example there would be something like holacracy or systems of record.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:52.760
So when you have software that sort of underlies all of the decisions in coordination,

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.470
um, it really changes the way work is done.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:59.090
So that's kind of the historical trend that we're seeing.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:02.420
This is relevant because most of, um, society, right,

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:06.860
is organized around these large-scale organizations and as they're changing,

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.425
then the structures of society are changing too.

00:06:09.425 --> 00:06:11.205
So I'm going to, um,

00:06:11.205 --> 00:06:15.985
use this as the motivating quote for the remainder of the presentation.

00:06:15.985 --> 00:06:20.780
So, Jerry Davis, having studied the vanishing corporation in the United States,

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.930
um, has this sort of provocative way of thinking about this.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:28.945
He says that organizational design is now a subfield of computer science.

00:06:28.945 --> 00:06:32.000
So I'm gonna give you some examples of why he says that,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.500
sort of what it means,

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:38.850
and then how this idea of using tools and tech for team design fit into this idea.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:47.745
So the first example of organizational design,

00:06:47.745 --> 00:06:50.064
now being a subfield of computer science,

00:06:50.064 --> 00:06:52.115
is the web-based enterprise.

00:06:52.115 --> 00:06:55.490
So let's start with back at the example of Circuit City.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.595
So what was going on there.

00:06:57.595 --> 00:07:01.340
So the technology that underlies this change is

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.790
actually not very advanced or revolutionary at this point.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.440
Um, as Jerry points out,

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.780
it's something simple called the, um,

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.360
Application Programming Interface, and all that means is

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.205
the way that the front half of the website talks to the back half of the website.

00:07:16.205 --> 00:07:21.470
So you'll have a customer inquiry on the website that can now do a query to the database.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:26.010
So that was something that used to require a white-collar professional who would check,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.590
you know, check that customer inquiry and then look at the database.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.815
And so that's now something that is automated.

00:07:30.815 --> 00:07:35.605
So his point is that with the API doing a lot of the coordination work,

00:07:35.605 --> 00:07:40.430
um, we're going to see an increase in what he calls the webpage enterprise.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.000
So I'm gonna use his favorite example here.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.830
This is kind of a whimsical example.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:49.255
It's a new kind of consumer cult product called the Instant Pot.

00:07:49.255 --> 00:07:52.210
So the point with the Instant Pot is,

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:55.200
um, this was a new, um,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.075
product category on, um,

00:07:57.075 --> 00:08:01.875
Amazon that now has over 4- almost 40,000 five-star reviews.

00:08:01.875 --> 00:08:05.800
And what's interesting about this is the Instant Pot started with

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.585
a staff of five people and an investment of $350,000.

00:08:10.585 --> 00:08:14.515
So they were able to weave together this ecosystem of

00:08:14.515 --> 00:08:18.070
online Internet resources to create,

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:19.990
develop, produce, manufacture, ship,

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.675
sale, uh, sell, and market this new product.

00:08:23.675 --> 00:08:29.670
So I'm gonna, um, call out this New Yorker article from 2013 by

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.740
Nathan Heller that sort of explains what's going on here and why

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:36.320
we can expect to see an increase in the webpage enterprise.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.015
So Nathan Heller published this in 2013 after interviewing

00:08:40.015 --> 00:08:43.780
some people who we're working the Silicon Valley, San Francisco Market.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:48.060
And his informant was explaining this and saying in 2005,

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.365
the whole thing exploded.

00:08:49.365 --> 00:08:53.205
Did you need hardware? No. Now you just put it on Amazon or Rackspace.

00:08:53.205 --> 00:08:55.665
Software? Now it's all open-source.

00:08:55.665 --> 00:08:58.560
Distribution? It's just the App Store, it's Facebook.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.340
Customer service? That's just Twitter.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.075
You just respond to your best customers on Twitter and get satisfaction.

00:09:04.075 --> 00:09:07.630
Sales and marketing? Now it's Google Words, AdSense.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.450
So the cost to build and launch a product went from five million to

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.955
one million to 500,000, now it's 50,000.

00:09:13.955 --> 00:09:18.450
So the- the revolutionary technology behind all of this is the API.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.290
It's a very simple technology but it makes, um,

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.805
coordination and decision-making much easier and automated.

00:09:24.805 --> 00:09:27.405
And we're seeing this used in a lot of different,

00:09:27.405 --> 00:09:30.180
um, organizational applications now.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:34.460
So I was giving you an example of the webpage enterprise.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.780
And now let's get into team design which is the motivation of this webinar.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:43.115
So in this whole arc of talking about how technology changes organizations,

00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:48.835
what we're seeing now is that you can use APIs basically to interface with the workforce.

00:09:48.835 --> 00:09:53.140
So we see that in crowdsourcing and we see that in the flash teams research.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.460
Let me illustrate a little bit about how that works.

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:00.000
So several years ago,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.670
um, a PhD student, Daniela Retelny,

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:06.480
a Computer Science Professor Michael Bernstein and a, uh,

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.515
group of other PhD students- master students and I did research,

00:10:10.515 --> 00:10:14.330
um, to develop a web platform called Foundry.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:17.195
So the thing to think about with Foundry,

00:10:17.195 --> 00:10:21.555
it's a- it's a web platform that- that allows team design.

00:10:21.555 --> 00:10:24.200
So anybody can come to Foundry which is

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:28.450
an open-source platform and can basically author a flash team.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:34.565
And then Foundry interfaces with online labor markets where there are millions of people,

00:10:34.565 --> 00:10:39.380
millions of workers who are available and

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:44.800
registered in Upwork who can come work on the flash team.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.270
So I wanna talk about some of the features of

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:51.110
this platform and the way that it connects to this overall trend of

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:54.200
using more technology to do the coordination and decision

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.740
making that people and organizational charts were doing in the past.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:05.575
So an important component or feature of Foundry is

00:11:05.575 --> 00:11:10.090
that it interfaces with large online labor markets.

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:12.955
When we did this study several years ago,

00:11:12.955 --> 00:11:19.300
we configured Foundry to interface with a labor market called Upwork, so Upwork.com.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:23.200
So Upwork has millions of people who are expert in

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:29.020
various kinds of professions like web development, mobile app development.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:34.630
Um, we worked with people who are product designers, content creators, editors.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:38.110
We had people who did marketing, business plans.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:41.470
So basically the way that Foundry works is it will issue

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:46.360
an open call to the people on Upwork who have registered to work on a flash team.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:49.150
For the first research project that we did,

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:52.210
we were trying to demonstrate that this kind of approach

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:55.525
could be as fast as previous crowdsourcing approaches.

00:11:55.525 --> 00:12:00.205
So we can figured Foundry to hire first come first serve,

00:12:00.205 --> 00:12:05.005
um, which will obviously optimize on speed and the efficiency of hiring.

00:12:05.005 --> 00:12:07.030
And then I'll talk later about different ways to

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:10.445
configure the composing or assembling of a flash team.

00:12:10.445 --> 00:12:13.320
So basically Foundry interfaces using this sort of

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:17.550
like API approach to a large online labor market.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:22.885
And then, um, the people will click on the link,

00:12:22.885 --> 00:12:24.520
and then be brought to Foundry,

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:27.970
and then they'll see what their role is in the overall flash team.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:29.095
They'll see the workflow.

00:12:29.095 --> 00:12:31.090
They'll see what their first task is.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:35.230
They'll see the way that their task is interdependent with other people on the team.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:37.630
They can upload deliverables which will automatically

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.360
pass them along to other people on the team.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:43.780
And then there are different features for the management of the flash team

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:46.885
which will show like what work is on time,

00:12:46.885 --> 00:12:49.180
what work is, um, at risk,

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:53.000
and, uh, the different ways that people can talk to each other.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:58.270
The other feature of Foundry that is

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:02.740
interesting for managers who are thinking about team design,

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:07.285
is that we configured it so that it could be basically like

00:13:07.285 --> 00:13:13.070
an organizational chart that anybody who was working on the flash team could edit.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:16.380
So what I'm picturing here on the bottom left,

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.300
so think of that as a website,

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:22.280
a web portal that anyone on the team has access to.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:26.500
So you see sort of the participants of the team down the left side,

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:30.310
and then the different tasks that they are part of kind of next to them.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:33.070
So the way that this works is anyone can make

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:37.000
a copy of the organizational chart or the workflow,

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:38.905
and then they can make a suggestion.

00:13:38.905 --> 00:13:43.510
So they'll basically add a task or add a role in their copy,

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.475
and then when they merge it back in,

00:13:45.475 --> 00:13:49.285
then everyone else will see what their suggestion is.

00:13:49.285 --> 00:13:53.395
So what's a cool property about this is it allows for

00:13:53.395 --> 00:13:57.880
top-down or bottom-up recommendations to how to change the team design,

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:00.445
or how to change the organizational chart.

00:14:00.445 --> 00:14:06.850
So if somebody on the frontline sees that their team is maybe needing another person, um,

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:10.900
or if they see that the hierarchy isn't working the way that it's configured,

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:12.850
then they can make a suggestion on

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:16.675
the org chart and when they merge it back in then somebody will review it.

00:14:16.675 --> 00:14:20.680
They'll review what the suggestion is and they can accept the suggestion,

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:22.600
change it, reject it,

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:25.240
um, and then the suggestion will appear on

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:29.990
everybody else's page for how the work is going to be structured going forward.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:34.045
So again, there's lots of different ways that you could configure this.

00:14:34.045 --> 00:14:38.680
You can imagine what would happen if you made this sort of like a democratic vote.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:40.990
Like, let's say that somebody makes a suggestion

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:42.910
that the hierarchy should be switched up for

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:45.010
some portion of the task and then everyone on

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:47.275
the team could sort of vote if that's what should happen.

00:14:47.275 --> 00:14:50.905
So you can have this democratic review of the suggestion.

00:14:50.905 --> 00:14:53.590
The way that we had it configured during the study was to

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.080
have the managers were the ones who always,

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:59.320
um, approved the different suggestions that were edited in.

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:03.400
So again, it's interesting to think about this as the platform is now

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:07.075
something that is dynamically editable by anyone on the team,

00:15:07.075 --> 00:15:11.170
and that sort of changes the properties of organizational structures,

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:15.590
organizational design, and also the way that those decisions get made.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.545
So we ran a proof of concept study to show that

00:15:23.545 --> 00:15:28.025
this kind of approach could be used to crowdsource really complex projects.

00:15:28.025 --> 00:15:29.970
So what I'm illustrating here are

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.130
the different organizational structures that

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:35.220
emerged in the three different studies that we ran.

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:37.890
So each of these started with just the,

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.290
sort of, um, person who's our client,

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:41.520
the person who had the idea,

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.375
so illustrated in the top is sort of this black figurehead.

00:15:45.375 --> 00:15:50.365
So it started like that, and then over time they would add a different, um,

00:15:50.365 --> 00:15:52.060
role, a different team,

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:56.720
a different hierarchy, and these are the organizational structures that emerged.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:16:01.690
And I'll show here the workflows that emerged.

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.675
So each of these tasks was added over time.

00:16:04.675 --> 00:16:09.760
At the colors of the task boxes illustrate the different teams.

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:13.090
So for example, in the top,

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:16.165
you're looking at one of the study- one of the projects that we ran,

00:16:16.165 --> 00:16:19.465
and you can see that towards the end of it that's when the QA,

00:16:19.465 --> 00:16:21.610
which is an orange, the quality assurance team

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:24.980
came online and started doing all of their tasks.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:28.930
So the other thing that's interesting to think about here with

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:33.070
flash teams and with this kind of archival record of all the work that was done,

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:37.660
is the kind of data science that can be done on the tasks,

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:40.720
the roles, the interactions, the hierarchy.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:44.890
So this starts to enable a lot more data science or sort of

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:49.945
empirical analysis of team design of how the work is structured,

00:16:49.945 --> 00:16:53.360
um, of how sort of the work unfolds over time.

00:16:57.270 --> 00:16:59.815
So I illustrated, uh,

00:16:59.815 --> 00:17:02.305
the web page enterprise,

00:17:02.305 --> 00:17:04.735
and then flash teams.

00:17:04.735 --> 00:17:07.030
So web page enterprise you're using,

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:11.275
um, API to sort of connect different components of a website.

00:17:11.275 --> 00:17:13.840
With flash teams, you are connecting

00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:16.930
a client or somebody who has ideas with a whole team of

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:19.180
people using an online labor market and

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:23.020
a flash team using a flash team platform like Foundry.

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:26.260
What I want to talk about now is some of the later research that we

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:30.430
did after we ran the flash teams experiments,

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:35.680
and where we were continuing to see what was possible when you use kind of software,

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:39.950
when you use platforms to do some of this kind of organizing work.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:46.975
So the next example I want to talk about is different ways that you can use software to,

00:17:46.975 --> 00:17:53.180
to, to sort of think through the flash team members who's going to be on the team.

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:59.440
So the first example that I showed we were optimizing on kind of speed

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:05.200
and efficiency because we were in conversation with the crowdsourcing research community.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:07.945
I want to talk now about, um,

00:18:07.945 --> 00:18:11.830
another model that was developed by Niloufar Salehi

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:16.060
who is now a professor at UC Berkeley in the CS department.

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:20.140
So she was the PhD student who led this research.

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:22.585
And basically her idea, um,

00:18:22.585 --> 00:18:28.450
was to notice that the work of flash teams and flash organizations was basically

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:34.840
sacrificing some of the familiarity of team members in order to get speed.

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:39.775
So if you're just doing a first come first serve to a large labor market,

00:18:39.775 --> 00:18:41.995
that means you're just gonna get whose available,

00:18:41.995 --> 00:18:45.805
you're going to get fast staffing of the team.

00:18:45.805 --> 00:18:47.905
What she was wondering is,

00:18:47.905 --> 00:18:51.010
was there a way that you could staff the team to

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:55.130
really focus on the relationships between the team members?

00:18:55.230 --> 00:19:00.640
In general, this kind of model could be configured in several different ways.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:04.330
You could maybe be optimizing for different kinds of expertise.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:06.670
You could be optimizing for different time zones,

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:09.535
different availability, different experience levels.

00:19:09.535 --> 00:19:12.385
So the variables that we're optimizing here are

00:19:12.385 --> 00:19:15.865
the speed that we demonstrated with flash teams in the past,

00:19:15.865 --> 00:19:18.115
and then the familiarity,

00:19:18.115 --> 00:19:19.840
with people working together,

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:23.990
and sort of accumulating a lot of experience working together over time.

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:32.170
So basically, what she wanted to do with this study was to develop a system,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:35.050
an algorithmic system that would assemble or staff,

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:38.995
the Flash Teams in a way that optimized the relationships of the past.

00:19:38.995 --> 00:19:42.700
So team members who had worked together before were weighted more

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:47.440
heavily when staffing the next Flash Team that was, um, being developed.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:50.935
So she developed another platform called Huddler,

00:19:50.935 --> 00:19:53.170
and Huddler would keep track of all of

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:55.240
the times that people had worked together in the past,

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:58.795
and then use that information to staff the team's going forward.

00:19:58.795 --> 00:20:01.495
So we ran a study this is, um,

00:20:01.495 --> 00:20:04.595
published and available on her website.

00:20:04.595 --> 00:20:07.560
So we ran a study that basically showed that using

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:12.675
the Huddler algorithm that optimized for both familiarity and availability,

00:20:12.675 --> 00:20:17.725
we were able to quickly staff Flash Teams,

00:20:17.725 --> 00:20:20.365
where team members had worked together in the past,

00:20:20.365 --> 00:20:24.230
which is one of the predictors of really effective teams.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.970
All right. I want to now give

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:36.565
my final example of how organizational design is now a subfield of computer science.

00:20:36.565 --> 00:20:42.650
So this is a continuation of the Flash Teams' research stream.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:49.630
So if you think about this kind of over all or idea you have a platform that interfaces

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:55.375
with labor markets or even large internal populations of employees,

00:20:55.375 --> 00:20:58.600
and then convenes teams to work together on a project.

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:01.660
So there's different features that we're talking about here.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.615
One is the Assembly Feature,

00:21:04.615 --> 00:21:09.340
which is figuring out who should be on the team and sort of assembling them together.

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:15.610
Um, the second piece is how the platform can help people work together more effectively.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:19.240
So this last example that I'm giving is,

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:22.435
um, was led by a PhD student named Sharon Jew.

00:21:22.435 --> 00:21:25.810
And she was looking at how the platform could

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:30.410
interact with team members to help them work together more effectively.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:35.530
So this is the paper that she led.

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:37.630
She talked about it as,

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:40.165
um, searching for the Dream Team.

00:21:40.165 --> 00:21:43.780
So I like this visual because what this is showing basically is

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:49.370
the idea of how the platform helps the team members work together effectively.

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:53.490
So basically, what you're seeing here is three different teams.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:56.400
You have A, B, C. And then on the left,

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:57.450
you have different kinds of

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:01.805
team structures that the teams could use to coordinate their work.

00:22:01.805 --> 00:22:07.030
So you could have a centralized hierarchy or a decentralized hierarchy.

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:11.065
You could have different ways that the team members are interacting with each other.

00:22:11.065 --> 00:22:15.040
They could have like, an emergent kind of interaction pattern.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:19.915
They could have some sort of like round robin or equally distributed interaction pattern.

00:22:19.915 --> 00:22:24.805
So what you see illustrated here is at the 10 rounds that the teams worked together,

00:22:24.805 --> 00:22:27.595
they adopted different team structures

00:22:27.595 --> 00:22:30.310
based on the algorithm that I will talk about in just a minute,

00:22:30.310 --> 00:22:33.530
that was recommending what structures they should be using.

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:41.365
So what's interesting about this research I think in particular is it is

00:22:41.365 --> 00:22:44.110
a really nice illustration of the kind of

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:48.400
technical innovation that's needed for this kind of work.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:50.830
And then the kind of basic social science or like

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:54.355
managerial intuition that's needed for this kind of work.

00:22:54.355 --> 00:23:00.190
So what Sharon was sort of speaking to when she was leading this project is

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:02.560
a sort of known body of research in

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:07.165
organizational design or management about how to structure teams.

00:23:07.165 --> 00:23:10.300
And what all of this research has shown is that there's basically not

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.300
a universally ideal way to structure a team.

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:16.840
It's gonna depend on who the team members are,

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:18.370
what kind of task they're working on,

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:21.700
is it like an execution task or an innovation task?

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:25.164
So knowing this and sort of being familiar with this research,

00:23:25.164 --> 00:23:28.675
she developed this algorithmic approach that was going to

00:23:28.675 --> 00:23:33.055
explore all the different spaces that teams could use to structure,

00:23:33.055 --> 00:23:35.500
and then recommend different ones to,

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:38.440
um, the team leads, which are the Managers.

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:42.265
She ran an experiment that showed that

00:23:42.265 --> 00:23:47.275
the teams that were sort of running with this Dream Team system,

00:23:47.275 --> 00:23:49.630
um, outperformed the Manager lead teams or

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:53.870
the self-managed teams by about 40 percent in this one experiment.

00:23:56.700 --> 00:24:00.640
So the different structures that could

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:03.760
be recommended to the teams are sort of, um, listed here.

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:06.070
So there are a bunch of different ones that they could look at.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:09.205
Um, I talked about hierarchy and interaction patterns.

00:24:09.205 --> 00:24:12.070
I'll notice at the bottom there's one feedback norms,

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.920
so people could give- could be encouraged to recommend it to get

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:18.295
really encouraging feedback or really critical feedback.

00:24:18.295 --> 00:24:21.640
So this is sort of the space of all the different structures that

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:25.570
the teams could use to facilitate or coordinate their work.

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:27.760
This- this is sort of a decision space that

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.655
the managers and team members were exploring to figure out,

00:24:30.655 --> 00:24:32.260
how they should be working together,

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:34.375
how they shou- should structure their work.

00:24:34.375 --> 00:24:36.700
So the way this worked is, um,

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:40.285
she configured basically a Slack Bot.

00:24:40.285 --> 00:24:45.310
And the Slack Bot was- so the team members would come into Slack,

00:24:45.310 --> 00:24:50.350
and they would be interacting with each other and they would play a game for one round.

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.300
At the end of the round, um,

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:54.730
the Slack Bot would collect feedback from all of

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:57.400
the team members about how things had been going,

00:24:57.400 --> 00:25:02.440
and then using that data the Slack Bot would then recommend that they stay

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:08.360
with the same structure that they had before or try and experiment with a new structure.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:11.740
So you can see an example here.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:15.175
Um, this is one of the actual teams that was running,

00:25:15.175 --> 00:25:18.460
so you can see that team member is kinda joking around and he's saying,

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.560
"I'm too scared to type anything."

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:26.230
And then the Slack Bot recommends that they adopt a very,

00:25:26.230 --> 00:25:30.520
uh, cheerful encouraging way of giving feedback to each other.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:33.250
And then after that the team members, again,

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:35.440
this is kind of joking and interaction with the Bot,

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:36.700
the team members are saying like,

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.470
"Go team. We're killing it."

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:43.375
So there's kind of like feedback, um, evaluation,

00:25:43.375 --> 00:25:47.380
recommendation feedback loop is the kind of thing that we're thinking about here.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:50.335
And what the algorithm was doing to sort of, um,

00:25:50.335 --> 00:25:53.965
run the Bot behind the scenes was figure out if

00:25:53.965 --> 00:25:58.940
the Bot should recommend staying with the same structure or trying a new structure.

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:03.430
And what Sharon found in a study is basically

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:08.815
when- so the Dream Team teams outperformed the other teams.

00:26:08.815 --> 00:26:12.355
So when she dug into so- some of the dynamics of what was happening there,

00:26:12.355 --> 00:26:16.360
she saw that the managers- when the managers were running the team,

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:18.145
they weren't trying enough variations.

00:26:18.145 --> 00:26:22.330
They sort of found a structure that worked and just stayed there,

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:24.115
stayed with that structure.

00:26:24.115 --> 00:26:27.250
So the Dream Teams outperformed

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:32.320
the manager-lead teams because they explored more- they explored more structures.

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:34.720
And then these, uh,

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:39.070
self-managed teams are the teams that kept getting recommendations about how to change,

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:41.770
adopted the changed too often,

00:26:41.770 --> 00:26:44.260
which gave the- the team members started to ignore

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:47.935
the recommendations because it was too many recommendations and too many changes.

00:26:47.935 --> 00:26:53.110
So the Dream Team algorithm had this way of sort of figuring out how

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:55.750
much to explore versus how much to- to sort of stay

00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:59.320
with the same structure that had been working in the past.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:04.015
So in general, the Dream Team, uh,

00:27:04.015 --> 00:27:08.770
Bot interface is just this proof of concept of the idea that now that teams are

00:27:08.770 --> 00:27:13.675
increasingly working on platforms and everyone's sort of interacting in this platform,

00:27:13.675 --> 00:27:16.780
the platform can be configured to

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:21.790
have these kinds of like latent measurements of feedback or how things are going,

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:25.270
sort of a latent collection of data, analysis of data,

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:29.815
and then some sort of recommendation to try to help the teams perform better.

00:27:29.815 --> 00:27:33.730
Now, obviously across all of this there's going to be  um,

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:37.510
it's going to be really important that whatever platform,

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:40.390
whatever algorithm is being designed is done in

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:43.870
collaboration with the people who it's acting upon,

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:48.250
otherwise there will be a lot of kind of gaming or not using the system.

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:51.340
So it has to be something that is working for the people that

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:54.835
it's trying to help instead of some sort of like top-down tech design,

00:27:54.835 --> 00:27:58.730
which tends to get ignored subverted or gained.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:05.995
So those are the examples of how team design is now happening increasingly

00:28:05.995 --> 00:28:13.524
on platforms using data for assembling teams, for helping them coordinate,

00:28:13.524 --> 00:28:17.230
um, and how this sort of allows for different properties of the teams,

00:28:17.230 --> 00:28:18.730
they're happening, you know,

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:21.340
increasingly outside of organizations

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:25.340
or in these sort of optimized ways within organizations.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:30.520
So I wanted to conclude on this slide,

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:34.030
which is sort of what do you take away from all of

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:39.110
these kinds of studies and this is the kind of future of work conversation.

00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:44.230
And across the number of different studies that we've done or the different kinds of

00:28:44.230 --> 00:28:48.490
conversations that we've been having with different leaders of organizations,

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:50.215
here's sort of the takeaways.

00:28:50.215 --> 00:28:53.830
This is kind of where I see things- this is where I see

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:57.280
the conversations sort of happening and getting a little bit stuck in organizations,

00:28:57.280 --> 00:28:59.650
this is opportunities basically.

00:28:59.650 --> 00:29:03.115
So the first is, um,

00:29:03.115 --> 00:29:13.270
all of this data can be used for making decisions in kind of smarter more optimized ways,

00:29:13.270 --> 00:29:15.100
data can be helpful.

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:18.700
What I see is sort of the limit in a lot of organizations right now is

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:22.315
actually access to data or to data infrastructure.

00:29:22.315 --> 00:29:27.280
So a lot of the managers that I talk to will have- will sort of be bought into

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:33.040
this vision of how software and how data can be used for organizing and for coordination,

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:36.490
but basically it's hard to get it done in their company or in

00:29:36.490 --> 00:29:41.395
their organization just because the data infrastructure is not there,

00:29:41.395 --> 00:29:44.170
like people don't have access to data.

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:46.690
Um, they don't have- yeah,

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:48.460
the- the tools with the access to the data in

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:52.495
non-siloed ways to sort of do this kind of optimized decision-making.

00:29:52.495 --> 00:29:55.930
So investing in access to data and sort of like

00:29:55.930 --> 00:30:00.400
a really broad open data infrastructure seems to be,

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:03.550
um, a capability that a lot of people are working on right now.

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:08.230
Um, and then part of that is now that managers and

00:30:08.230 --> 00:30:12.985
different occupations are using data to do things like team design,

00:30:12.985 --> 00:30:16.030
team assembly to support team coordination,

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:20.515
or just other kinds of decision-making like market research or things like that.

00:30:20.515 --> 00:30:24.160
I'm seeing a lot of conversation about companies and

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.880
occupations needing to develop a new kind of data fluency.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:31.750
So people who were not necessarily having to use data for

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:35.230
the kinds of decisions they were making in the past are now needing like data curriculum.

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:37.120
What is- what is like an A/B test,

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:39.835
what is, you know, data science 101?

00:30:39.835 --> 00:30:43.780
Um, the second takeaway I think for managers, um,

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:48.910
that I'm seeing as a result of all of these technological changes is that

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:51.910
people are needing new processes

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:55.030
for thinking about how to manage the boundary of the firm.

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:58.720
What I mean by that is like Upwork is

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:03.625
this large online labor market of really expert people,

00:31:03.625 --> 00:31:06.550
and then there are companies that are

00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:09.835
interfacing with online labor markets or sort of like,

00:31:09.835 --> 00:31:12.955
I mean it's- it's a- it's another way of thinking about outsourcing.

00:31:12.955 --> 00:31:16.495
So I'm seeing a lot of conversation

00:31:16.495 --> 00:31:20.170
around how organizations can interface with the crowd,

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:22.120
can interface with online labor markets,

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:24.550
can interface with freelancers or

00:31:24.550 --> 00:31:27.625
people who are sort of working outside of the organization.

00:31:27.625 --> 00:31:30.520
There are some really important social implications and

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:34.120
policy implications that are part of a larger conversation.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:38.140
Um, for the purposes of- of this webinar, um,

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:40.120
what I'm seeing is that people are talking a lot

00:31:40.120 --> 00:31:42.250
about how to manage the boundary of the firm,

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:44.785
and how to sort of coordinate with the crowd.

00:31:44.785 --> 00:31:50.230
The last piece of it is a lot of this is new,

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:51.325
a lot of it is emerging,

00:31:51.325 --> 00:31:52.870
a lot of it is changing,

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:54.940
and there's a lot of cool opportunities.

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:59.065
So there's not really a "thou shalt" or like a right way to do this at this point.

00:31:59.065 --> 00:32:01.975
What I'm seeing be the most successful is, um,

00:32:01.975 --> 00:32:06.700
companies that are investing in kind of human-centered design around this and looking

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:12.490
for really kind of tight feedback loops in trying something, er,

00:32:12.490 --> 00:32:15.700
doing some sort of like co-design with workers,

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:18.430
with the employees and figuring out how to configure

00:32:18.430 --> 00:32:21.400
these things in a way that supports work,

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.020
and that supports workflows,

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:26.470
and helps people kind of do the kind of work in the way that they want

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:30.625
to in a really what we're- kind of virtuous feedback loop.

00:32:30.625 --> 00:32:34.450
So these are the kinds of problems that I'm seeing in a lot of the, um,

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:39.280
examples of how- of the conversations of how to sort of start to think

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:44.530
about using software for organizational design and team design in organizations.

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:48.740
And I look forward to, uh, continuing conversation.

00:32:48.990 --> 00:32:52.105
All right. Thank you so much Melissa, uh,

00:32:52.105 --> 00:32:54.475
for this very informative presentation,

00:32:54.475 --> 00:32:56.560
it's such a timely and important topic.

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:59.165
Um, I think it'll really help everyone.

00:32:59.165 --> 00:33:02.580
The first one is, "your paper on flash teams stated

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:05.970
that this method can be used for engineering and design work.

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:08.610
Um, but is there a limitation on the type of work

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:11.400
that these kinds of methods can be used on,

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:16.610
and do you plan any future research on different types of work?"

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:19.720
Yeah, thank you for that question.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.390
Um, that reminds me of something that I had wanted to mention actually.

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:25.960
Um, we're- so we're seeing a lot of different examples of

00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:30.790
now industry use cases of something like flash teams.

00:33:30.790 --> 00:33:33.880
So for example, there's a San Francisco startup called

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.795
Gigster that does software and engineering,

00:33:37.795 --> 00:33:40.345
um, so that's similar to what we were talking about.

00:33:40.345 --> 00:33:42.700
But there's actually a lot of other models that I'm seeing,

00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:46.495
um, some cool examples include there's something called Artella.

00:33:46.495 --> 00:33:51.580
Artella is artists, so they're digital artists who do, um,

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:54.460
they sort of have like the way that- the way that the founder

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:57.220
explained it to me is they have like the Pixar workflow but then they

00:33:57.220 --> 00:34:00.520
have like a community of people who do digital animation who

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:04.345
can come participate in the different projects that each other pitch.

00:34:04.345 --> 00:34:07.540
Um, there's something called Cataline which does

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:11.110
this kind of model for, um, business plans.

00:34:11.110 --> 00:34:13.210
So it's basically a network of like MBA's.

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:15.340
So if you need to do a flash business plan

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:18.280
[LAUGHTER] you can interface with MBA's who do this.

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:19.840
So, um, I've seen- I've seen it.

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:22.570
And I- I've, as I've given this presentation I've heard people have

00:34:22.570 --> 00:34:26.455
ideas for like flash teams of accountants, for like audits.

00:34:26.455 --> 00:34:30.490
So I think for proj- I think for project-based work we're gonna see

00:34:30.490 --> 00:34:34.480
an increase in this kind of idea of like platform-mediated projects.

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:36.295
So I think it works well for project work.

00:34:36.295 --> 00:34:38.155
I think the open question,

00:34:38.155 --> 00:34:40.255
and this is something that we've thought a lot about as well,

00:34:40.255 --> 00:34:47.110
the open question is, what does the sort of maintenance of whatever the deliverable was,

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:48.760
so if you have this like body of code,

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:52.810
if you have like the- the sort of like you know that code that- of the mobile app,

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:56.470
like who maintains that over time when you have this kind of flash model?

00:34:56.470 --> 00:35:01.780
So the idea of the like core pr- the core team that stays on and maintains

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:04.600
versus like the peripheral team that in a flash

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:07.660
comes and helps participate in building something out.

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:10.435
So the- the- I think that ends up being the- the,

00:35:10.435 --> 00:35:13.000
um, where we're thinking about,

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:15.370
where innovation would have to happen or like new thinking

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:17.770
would have to happen is in, uh,

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.140
just like the maintenance of longer projects,

00:35:20.140 --> 00:35:21.880
because this works best for the kinds of like

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:24.670
short flash projects that I've been talking about.

00:35:24.670 --> 00:35:28.960
Thanks. So here's a more general question.

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:31.960
So teams have always been used to get work done.

00:35:31.960 --> 00:35:33.895
So why aren't we better at it,

00:35:33.895 --> 00:35:37.705
and why does technology make a difference?

00:35:37.705 --> 00:35:40.135
Yeah. That's- so that's a great question.

00:35:40.135 --> 00:35:41.950
Um, I think that's an important one.

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:48.670
And, um, it's- what- what I'm thinking about with that question is when I assign,

00:35:48.670 --> 00:35:52.300
uh, team projects in my classes then it's- it's to groans.

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:54.835
It's met to groans, nobody likes working on a team

00:35:54.835 --> 00:35:58.690
because you end up with all the social problems of, you know,

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:02.395
maybe some people are working harder than other people, um,

00:36:02.395 --> 00:36:05.590
you know maybe you have some like funky hierarchy where the person who's

00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:08.995
most expert is not the one who's in- who's- who has the authority.

00:36:08.995 --> 00:36:15.565
Um, so all of the like social psychological problems and challenges of groups remain.

00:36:15.565 --> 00:36:18.460
Technology does not solve that at all.

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:20.545
[LAUGHTER] Sometimes technology can make it worse.

00:36:20.545 --> 00:36:23.200
We've seen like virtual teams and online teams

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:26.770
that research has shown tend to have more conflict than others.

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:29.275
Um, so I will say,

00:36:29.275 --> 00:36:31.300
you know sometimes there are- sometimes teams

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:34.000
can be better than working alone when you have like

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:37.120
a synergistic team where you're like bouncing ideas off

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:38.740
each other and- and you really like working

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:40.945
together in this innovative way, that's the dream.

00:36:40.945 --> 00:36:44.275
And I don't think that technology in any deterministic way

00:36:44.275 --> 00:36:47.650
gets you from like the bad team to the good team.

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:49.750
So the- the point is- the point is- is good.

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:51.400
[LAUGHTER] The- the point stands,

00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:53.560
teams are hard. [LAUGHTER]

00:36:53.560 --> 00:36:59.725
So has your research found any way to control for the quality of the final deliverable?

00:36:59.725 --> 00:37:01.290
Um.

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:04.270
So- so thank you.

00:37:04.270 --> 00:37:06.100
Again, that's a really good question.

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:13.795
Um, so, uh, when we did the flash teams, field experiment, um,

00:37:13.795 --> 00:37:18.010
one of the challenges that the clients- so

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:22.315
they were the ones who had the idea of what they would build using the flash team.

00:37:22.315 --> 00:37:28.600
So they were very concerned about the quality of the product that was being built.

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:31.795
And, um, what the feedback that we got from

00:37:31.795 --> 00:37:36.640
them is that that is definitely a place where,

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:39.760
um, the interface between an existing organization,

00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:42.055
where you oftentimes have a lot of,

00:37:42.055 --> 00:37:45.535
kind of like quality checks and quality assurance built in place.

00:37:45.535 --> 00:37:51.055
That's where that interface can be pretty problematic or place to really watch for.

00:37:51.055 --> 00:37:52.900
So again it's a really important question and I

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:55.330
think it's the right kind of thing to be thinking about.

00:37:55.330 --> 00:38:01.930
Um, in our study the- the solve for it was- was simply around hierarchy.

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:04.900
It was simply around having like managers being the ones who are

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:08.245
accountable for whatever it was turned in and for kind of checking it.

00:38:08.245 --> 00:38:13.630
Um, so I have seen kind of studies or I have

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:16.210
seen different proofs of concept where people are looking

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:19.240
at like technical solution for trying to look at,

00:38:19.240 --> 00:38:20.710
um, how to, you know,

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:24.055
assess quality and more like automated ways or whatever.

00:38:24.055 --> 00:38:26.635
Um, so in- in our instance we just used,

00:38:26.635 --> 00:38:27.925
uh, we just used managers.

00:38:27.925 --> 00:38:30.280
So the managers were accountable for the quality of the product,

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:32.185
with quality assurance teams,

00:38:32.185 --> 00:38:35.050
um, so that's- that's the,

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:36.790
uh, the kind of like old school way.

00:38:36.790 --> 00:38:40.705
Managers armed with technology sort of was the- was the fix.

00:38:40.705 --> 00:38:45.475
And, um, I think we'll see more of kind of studies of this in the future,

00:38:45.475 --> 00:38:48.950
because it is one of the main problems with crowdsourcing.

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:53.530
Great. So, um, you said something, uh,

00:38:53.530 --> 00:38:55.630
about accountability and setting

00:38:55.630 --> 00:38:58.075
it up and it reminded me of this question that was asked.

00:38:58.075 --> 00:39:02.860
So essentially, they're asking if leadership or

00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:08.290
managers ultimately make the decision to use a digital team,

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:10.975
should they also design the process,

00:39:10.975 --> 00:39:14.364
will the project teams do a better job,

00:39:14.364 --> 00:39:16.990
how do you integrate knowledge and decisions?

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:19.765
Um, yeah, good question.

00:39:19.765 --> 00:39:24.535
Um, so the managers should not design the team. I agree with you.

00:39:24.535 --> 00:39:27.640
I think- I think we are in- in radical agreement here.

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:31.225
Um, so I think one thing- so we had, uh,

00:39:31.225 --> 00:39:33.415
a flash team study, um,

00:39:33.415 --> 00:39:34.900
several- like five years ago,

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:37.225
I then the one I was presenting this, it's a little newer.

00:39:37.225 --> 00:39:40.360
Um, so the- the one we had done initially,

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:44.560
we used a- a workflow where everything was designed up front.

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:46.930
So that would be something more like a manager making

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:49.810
all the decisions for like how things should be structured.

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:52.240
For a very, very simple execution,

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:53.800
it was kind of fine,

00:39:53.800 --> 00:39:56.710
but what we saw is that like, you know,

00:39:56.710 --> 00:40:00.955
like one task into those flash teams the workflow was obsolete.

00:40:00.955 --> 00:40:03.520
This sort of like top-down design of how things should

00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:06.265
go was already like out-of-date, obsolete,

00:40:06.265 --> 00:40:11.620
and then the people who were actually doing the work had to improvise and they

00:40:11.620 --> 00:40:14.290
went very much like off platform and just improvised a better way

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:17.020
to do it that fit their style,

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:18.249
that fit their expertise,

00:40:18.249 --> 00:40:20.185
that fit the deliverable better.

00:40:20.185 --> 00:40:23.650
So that's actually what led us to work on

00:40:23.650 --> 00:40:27.940
the new functionality and Foundry that allows for this kind of editing over time.

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:31.465
So now a manager like the client who has the idea,

00:40:31.465 --> 00:40:34.840
basically just starts with like a blank slate and then hires people,

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:37.750
and then they themselves edit, add teams,

00:40:37.750 --> 00:40:41.470
add tasks, kind of decide what should happen over time.

00:40:41.470 --> 00:40:44.260
So it is much more emergent and much more kind of

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:47.785
team member designed together over time.

00:40:47.785 --> 00:40:50.710
There are- there were some challenges with that,

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:55.090
like we got feedback from the- from the team members that sometimes they

00:40:55.090 --> 00:41:00.610
wanted more direction from the client manager and sometimes they wanted less.

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:02.635
So it's still is a little bit of a balance.

00:41:02.635 --> 00:41:05.350
But the functionality that I would sort of look at there is

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:08.260
the idea of kind of like co-designing the workflow,

00:41:08.260 --> 00:41:13.820
co-creating the structures together over time with some sort of dynamic interface.

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:17.395
So we have, um,

00:41:17.395 --> 00:41:18.820
I was just re-reviewing.

00:41:18.820 --> 00:41:23.245
So we have several questions on concerns about IP and data.

00:41:23.245 --> 00:41:25.495
Um, so how do you, uh,

00:41:25.495 --> 00:41:29.705
how do you address those kinds of concerns when working on a flash team,

00:41:29.705 --> 00:41:32.340
or- or using a flash team for work?

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:33.825
Yeah. So that's a great question.

00:41:33.825 --> 00:41:36.750
So intellectual property is another really kind

00:41:36.750 --> 00:41:40.140
of key issue around this flash team model.

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:41.670
When we did the study,

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:45.900
we just followed the Upwork policy for intellectual property.

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:52.330
So Upwork has protections in place- protections and policies in place,

00:41:52.330 --> 00:41:55.480
that basically says that the client owns the IP.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:57.550
And there's some, um,

00:41:57.550 --> 00:41:59.485
there are some ways that they,

00:41:59.485 --> 00:42:01.870
through their sort of user agreement, uh,

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:06.355
deal with the IP issues of having freelancers work on,

00:42:06.355 --> 00:42:09.280
um, intellectual property of a company or a client.

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:13.120
Um, so it's- it's a little bit of a punting the question and just saying about the Up-

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:16.060
the Upwork labor market had their protections in

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:19.510
place and that's what we were relying on when we did the flash teams.

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:25.165
Um, I think as organizations become increasingly disintermediated,

00:42:25.165 --> 00:42:28.270
we see more of these kind of platform models

00:42:28.270 --> 00:42:32.095
and I suspect there will be changes to policy,

00:42:32.095 --> 00:42:36.610
um, I'm not an expert in that but maybe changes to the way that IP is protected.

00:42:36.610 --> 00:42:39.895
All right. This is- it's the right conversation and,

00:42:39.895 --> 00:42:43.630
um, I think the future might look different than what we have now.

00:42:43.630 --> 00:42:45.460
Okay. So I'm going to,

00:42:45.460 --> 00:42:47.725
uh, give you two more, uh,

00:42:47.725 --> 00:42:50.530
two more questions that are very,

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:53.095
uh, common themes through here.

00:42:53.095 --> 00:42:58.360
So the first one is how- how should people go about

00:42:58.360 --> 00:43:04.330
trying to get this kind of work started within a more mature organization,

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:07.810
how did they get people to be receptive to this,

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:10.960
not feel replaced, still feel like

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:15.740
their work is important and while also having these- these flash teams?

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:20.800
Um, so there's- there's

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:25.510
sort of two different concerns in that question and I think both are really valid.

00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:27.025
One is sort of, um,

00:43:27.025 --> 00:43:29.860
like how to have conversations in

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:35.725
large organizations that have an existing way of doing things and one is concerns about,

00:43:35.725 --> 00:43:38.560
uh, worker replacement and kind of like worker rights,

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:40.750
and I think both of those are really- really important.

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:45.040
Um, so I'm actually start with the first- the second one first.

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:52.705
Um, I think that there is a really broad conversation going on right now about how,

00:43:52.705 --> 00:43:57.100
um, kind of like this social fabric of our society and not to be dramatic,

00:43:57.100 --> 00:43:59.620
but kind of like the social fabric of our society.

00:43:59.620 --> 00:44:01.465
So for years and years,

00:44:01.465 --> 00:44:04.750
the social safety net has existed around organizations,

00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:09.190
people have had their entire careers in org- in like a single organization,

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:11.230
you know, health insurance,

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:14.229
retirement, pension, career ladders,

00:44:14.229 --> 00:44:19.120
like post-industrial revolution, society just evolved around organizations,

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:20.860
and as that changes,

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:24.220
then that means that there's a lot of- a lot more precarity.

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:26.185
And like a lot more precarity for workers,

00:44:26.185 --> 00:44:28.060
and again that's the right conversation,

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:30.520
that should be like the foreground of what we're talking about with

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:33.445
any of these like platform models for organizing.

00:44:33.445 --> 00:44:35.290
Um, so it's just such a larger conversation,

00:44:35.290 --> 00:44:36.475
thank you for bringing it up.

00:44:36.475 --> 00:44:40.000
Um, and I'll- I'll note that there are just- there are so many places that

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:43.855
conversations are happening and I- I hope we all continue to participate in that.

00:44:43.855 --> 00:44:48.039
Um, in terms of inside organizations,

00:44:48.039 --> 00:44:49.720
how to start this conversation,

00:44:49.720 --> 00:44:51.985
what I've seen is, um,

00:44:51.985 --> 00:44:54.430
that there are- are people who will hear

00:44:54.430 --> 00:44:56.830
something like this and they'll- they'll sort of recognize it,

00:44:56.830 --> 00:44:59.905
there's like some sort of data source and their organization

00:44:59.905 --> 00:45:04.225
that could be used for this kind of organizing.

00:45:04.225 --> 00:45:06.685
And- and then like a small pilot,

00:45:06.685 --> 00:45:09.490
like it's just a small proof of concept that shows

00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:12.670
how to use those data to assemble a team,

00:45:12.670 --> 00:45:15.565
or like a small pilot that shows how,

00:45:15.565 --> 00:45:17.785
um, I don't know like a- Slack.

00:45:17.785 --> 00:45:19.330
I mean Slack doesn't solve everything.

00:45:19.330 --> 00:45:21.835
I'm not- this is not me being tech deterministic.

00:45:21.835 --> 00:45:23.260
Um, but just I guess what I'm saying is like

00:45:23.260 --> 00:45:26.244
small pilots to demonstrate proof of concept,

00:45:26.244 --> 00:45:28.780
can start a really interesting conversation

00:45:28.780 --> 00:45:30.670
and also if you're interested in doing research,

00:45:30.670 --> 00:45:33.805
you can always contact, um, academics,

00:45:33.805 --> 00:45:36.070
see if you can do a study together because it sometimes that will

00:45:36.070 --> 00:45:38.560
be a low risk kind of outside of

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:41.170
the workflow that the operational workflow way that

00:45:41.170 --> 00:45:44.680
kind of explore these kind of ideas. That's like another idea.

00:45:44.680 --> 00:45:47.110
Okay. Last- last one.

00:45:47.110 --> 00:45:52.090
Um, so how do- how does ideation,

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:55.660
innovation and execution, integrate,

00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:58.855
um, to assign value for different contribution?

00:45:58.855 --> 00:46:00.700
So would you pay, you know,

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:02.785
if you had a base rate but then,

00:46:02.785 --> 00:46:06.310
certain people within the flash team were performing at

00:46:06.310 --> 00:46:10.750
a much higher rate or perhaps were not performing that well,

00:46:10.750 --> 00:46:13.670
would you then adjust the compensation?

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:26.920
Um, so it's an interesting question to think about kind of how people are,

00:46:26.920 --> 00:46:31.300
how people's contributions are recognized in different ways.

00:46:31.300 --> 00:46:35.065
Um, I'll tell you things that I've heard that I'm not satisfied by,

00:46:35.065 --> 00:46:36.490
but I think, um,

00:46:36.490 --> 00:46:38.800
and then I'll [LAUGHTER] and then I'll say what I think.

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:40.780
Um, so there is- well I shouldn't say it like that.

00:46:40.780 --> 00:46:43.930
So in terms of like- because what I would wanna avoid with

00:46:43.930 --> 00:46:47.425
this conversation is basically anything that is like again, like,

00:46:47.425 --> 00:46:49.090
too like tech deterministic,

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:56.280
like people are always interesting in how they will enact these different social systems.

00:46:56.280 --> 00:46:59.145
Um, so what I've heard people talk about is like,

00:46:59.145 --> 00:47:04.025
is different kinds of like micro equity or sort of like- some sort of like,

00:47:04.025 --> 00:47:06.970
you know, how do you do like equity in a flash team.

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:08.650
